ByteBuffer.allocateDirect on the wasm-gc target, its bytes zero.

Java has every element of a new buffer zero.  TeaVM's wasm-gc target takes a
direct buffer's bytes from its heap in linear memory as they are: what a
released block held, or the free list's own bookkeeping that the block was
carved from, so that a buffer of a few hundred bytes seldom starts out zero.
LWJGL's GL.createCapabilities, among others, counts on it: it skips a
function whose slot in its address buffer is not zero.

test/teavm/fixes/directbuffer.

--- a/teavm-classlib/org/teavm/classlib/java/nio/TByteBuffer.java
+++ b/teavm-classlib/org/teavm/classlib/java/nio/TByteBuffer.java
@@ -54,6 +54,8 @@ public abstract class TByteBuffer extends TBuffer implements TComparable<TByteBu
             if (addr == null) {
                 throw new TOutOfMemoryError();
             }
+            // the heap's blocks come as they were left
+            Address.fillZero(addr, capacity);
             var result = new TByteBufferWasmGC(null, null, 0, null, addr, capacity, false);
             result.regKey = JSObjects.create();
             result.regToken = JSObjects.create();
